    const Section(
      title: 'History & Systems Review:',
      content: 'Key areas to assess:\n- **Onset and progression**: Insidious vs. traumatic onset. Duration of symptoms. Which movements were lost first? Night pain severity.\n- **Diabetes history**: Duration, glycemic control (HbA1c), complications. Diabetes is the strongest risk factor for adhesive capsulitis — prevalence 10-36% in diabetics vs. 2-5% in general population. Bilateral involvement more common in diabetics.\n- **Other risk factors**: Thyroid disease (present), prior shoulder surgery or immobilization, Dupuytren contracture, cardiovascular disease, autoimmune conditions.\n- **Contralateral shoulder**: History of frozen shoulder on the left? Bilateral involvement occurs in 20-40% of cases (even higher with diabetes). Having one frozen shoulder increases risk of contralateral involvement.\n- **Red flags to exclude**: Night sweats, weight loss, history of cancer (rule out Pancoast tumor). Fever (septic arthritis). Progressive neurologic symptoms.\n- **Cervical spine symptoms**: Neck pain, radiculopathy symptoms — cervical pathology can refer pain to the shoulder and coexist.\n- **Functional impact**: ADLs (dressing, hygiene, grooming), work limitations (overhead filing, computer use), sleep quality, and psychological impact.\n- **Prior treatments**: Physical therapy, injections, medications tried and their effects.',
