      content: '**Vitals**: BP 128/82 mmHg, HR 76 bpm, Temp 36.8C, BMI 31.\n\n**Right Shoulder Examination**:\n- **Inspection**: No visible atrophy, swelling, or ecchymosis. Normal scapular position at rest.\n- **Active ROM** (compared to left/normal):\n  - Forward flexion: 95 degrees (left: 170 degrees)\n  - Abduction: 80 degrees (left: 175 degrees)\n  - External rotation (arm at side): 15 degrees (left: 60 degrees)\n  - Internal rotation: Hand to lateral thigh only (left: hand to T10 spinous process)\n- **Passive ROM**: Essentially identical to active ROM — passive motion does NOT significantly exceed active motion. This is the hallmark finding of adhesive capsulitis. Firm, "rubbery" end-feel in all directions.\n- **Capsular pattern present**: External rotation most limited, then abduction, then flexion, then internal rotation.\n- **Impingement tests**: Neer test — limited by ROM restriction, not clearly positive. Hawkins-Kennedy — limited by ROM restriction. **Negative** for classical impingement pain pattern.\n- **Rotator cuff strength** (tested within available ROM): Supraspinatus (empty can) — 4+/5, pain-limited. External rotation — 4+/5. Internal rotation — 5/5. These are relatively preserved, arguing against significant rotator cuff tear.\n- **Scapular motion**: Excessive scapulothoracic substitution noted during attempted abduction (early shrug sign) — compensating for glenohumeral restriction.\n- **Cervical spine**: Full ROM. Spurling test negative. No radicular symptoms.\n- **Left shoulder**: Full painless ROM. No signs of contralateral involvement at this time.\n- **Neurovascular**: Distal pulses intact. Sensation intact in C5-T1 distributions. No signs of thoracic outlet syndrome.',
