      title: '+ Describe the stages of adhesive capsulitis, the key differential diagnoses, and when imaging is indicated.',
      content: '**Stages of Adhesive Capsulitis** (Reeves Classification):\n\n**Stage 1 — Freezing (Painful Phase)**: Duration 2-9 months\n- Gradual onset of diffuse shoulder pain, often worse at night\n- Progressive loss of ROM begins\n- Pain is the predominant symptom\n- Arthroscopy: Diffuse synovitis with hypervascular synovium\n\n**Stage 2 — Frozen (Stiffening Phase)**: Duration 4-12 months\n- Pain may gradually decrease\n- Stiffness becomes the predominant complaint\n- ROM reaches its nadir\n- Arthroscopy: Dense, fibrotic capsular contracture with less synovitis\n\n**Stage 3 — Thawing (Resolution Phase)**: Duration 12-42 months\n- Gradual spontaneous improvement in ROM\n- Pain minimal\n- Recovery may be incomplete, especially in diabetics\n\n**This patient** is in the **late Stage 1/early Stage 2 transition** — she has significant pain AND progressive stiffness over 4 months.\n\n**Total natural history**: 1-3 years to resolution. However, in diabetics, recovery is often longer and less complete — up to 20% have permanent residual stiffness.\n\n**Key Differential Diagnoses**:\n1. **Rotator cuff tear**: Active ROM may be limited, but passive ROM is typically preserved (unlike adhesive capsulitis). Weakness on strength testing. Positive impingement signs.\n2. **Glenohumeral osteoarthritis**: Crepitus on exam. Bone-on-bone end-feel vs. capsular end-feel. Radiographic joint space narrowing and osteophytes.\n3. **Calcific tendinitis**: Acute onset pain, calcification on radiograph. May coexist with adhesive capsulitis.\n4. **Cervical radiculopathy**: Neck pain, dermatomal symptoms, positive Spurling test. Shoulder ROM is typically preserved.\n5. **Pancoast tumor**: Shoulder pain in a smoker, Horner syndrome, neurologic deficits — must exclude especially in patients with risk factors.\n6. **Posterior shoulder dislocation**: Locked internal rotation. Often missed. Best seen on axillary view radiograph.\n\n**Imaging Indications**:\n- **Plain radiographs (AP and axillary lateral)**: First-line. Usually normal in adhesive capsulitis but essential to rule out fracture, arthritis, calcific tendinitis, dislocation, and tumor. Mild osteopenia of the humeral head may be seen.\n- **MRI**: Not routinely required for diagnosis but indicated if rotator cuff tear is suspected, symptoms are atypical, or there is no improvement after 3-6 months of appropriate treatment. In adhesive capsulitis, MRI may show thickened inferior glenohumeral ligament, thickened axillary recess, and coracohumeral ligament thickening.\n- **Ultrasound**: Can evaluate rotator cuff integrity and guide injections. Doppler may show hypervascularity in the rotator interval in early stages.\n- **Arthrography**: Historically diagnostic (reduced joint capsule volume <10 mL, normal is 20-30 mL). Now rarely performed for diagnosis alone but used therapeutically in hydrodilatation.',
