      title: '+ Outline your comprehensive treatment plan including physical therapy, injection options, and indications for procedural interventions.',
      content: '**Treatment Plan — Staged Approach**:\n\n**1. Patient Education (Critical Foundation)**:\n- Explain the natural history — this condition WILL improve, but the timeline is months to years, not weeks.\n- Set expectations: Pain control is the priority in the freezing phase. Aggressive stretching during the painful phase can worsen inflammation and pain.\n- Reassure that most patients recover meaningful function.\n\n**2. Physical Therapy Program**:\n- **Freezing phase (current)**: Gentle ROM within pain tolerance. Pendulum exercises (Codman exercises). Passive and active-assisted ROM in pain-free range. Scapular stabilization exercises. Heat before therapy, ice after.\n- **Frozen phase**: Progressive stretching program — sustained low-load stretching at end-range. Wall slides, table slides, behind-the-back towel stretches, cross-body adduction stretch, sleeper stretch for internal rotation. Hold stretches 30-60 seconds, repeat 3-5 times, perform 2-3 times daily.\n- **Thawing phase**: Progressive strengthening as ROM improves. Rotator cuff and scapular stabilizer strengthening. Functional activity retraining.\n- **Frequency**: PT 2-3 times per week with daily home exercise program.\n\n**3. Pharmacologic Management**:\n- **NSAIDs**: Meloxicam 15 mg daily or naproxen 500 mg BID with meals — for pain and inflammation. Monitor renal function given diabetes. Limit to 2-4 weeks.\n- **Short course oral corticosteroid**: Prednisolone 30 mg daily x 3 weeks with taper — may provide rapid pain relief in the freezing phase, but blood glucose will need close monitoring given HbA1c 8.2%.\n- **Neuropathic adjuvant**: Gabapentin 300 mg at bedtime if night pain is severely disrupting sleep (titrate up as needed).\n\n**4. Injection Therapy**:\n- **Intra-articular glenohumeral corticosteroid injection**: First-line procedural intervention. Triamcinolone 40 mg with 4 mL lidocaine via posterior approach (or ultrasound-guided). Most effective in Stage 1 (freezing). Provides 4-6 weeks of pain relief to facilitate PT. Limit to 3 injections total. Warn patient of glucose elevation for 48-72 hours post-injection.\n- **Suprascapular nerve block**: Alternative or adjunct — blocks 70% of shoulder pain sensation. Useful for severe pain limiting participation in PT.\n- **Hydrodilatation (distension arthrography)**: Injection of saline (20-30 mL) and corticosteroid under fluoroscopic or ultrasound guidance to distend and rupture the contracted capsule. Evidence supports short-term ROM improvement. Consider if not responding to standard injection and PT after 3-4 months.\n\n**5. Indications for More Aggressive Intervention**:\n- **Manipulation under anesthesia (MUA)**: Consider if no improvement after 6+ months of conservative management. Performed under general anesthesia with controlled sequential stretching. Risk of fracture (especially in osteoporotic or diabetic patients), labral tear, rotator cuff tear, and nerve injury. Typically followed by immediate aggressive PT.\n- **Arthroscopic capsular release**: Alternative to MUA with better visualization and controlled release of the contracted capsule. Preferred in diabetic patients (who have thicker capsular fibrosis). Combined with MUA in some protocols.\n\n**6. Home Program**:\n- Pendulum exercises 3 times daily\n- Self-stretching with wand/cane exercises\n- Moist heat before stretching\n- Ice after exercise if painful\n- Sleep positioning: Avoid lying on the affected side. Pillow support under the affected arm.',
