    const Section(
      title: '+ How does diabetes management affect frozen shoulder outcomes, and what workplace modifications would you recommend?',
      content: '**Diabetes and Adhesive Capsulitis**:\n\n**Impact on Disease Course**:\n- Diabetic patients have **longer symptom duration**, **more severe stiffness**, and **less complete recovery** than non-diabetic patients.\n- HbA1c >7.5% (this patient is 8.2%) is associated with worse outcomes and higher recurrence rates.\n- Advanced glycation end-products (AGEs) accumulate in collagen tissue, increasing capsular fibrosis and stiffness.\n- Response to corticosteroid injections is often less robust and shorter-lasting in diabetics.\n- Bilateral involvement occurs in up to 40% of diabetic patients — monitor the left shoulder.\n\n**Diabetes Optimization Recommendations**:\n1. **Coordinate with endocrinologist/PCP**: Emphasize that improving glycemic control may directly improve shoulder outcomes. Target HbA1c <7.0%.\n2. **Monitor glucose around treatments**: Intra-articular steroid injections and oral steroids will elevate blood glucose significantly for 48-72 hours. Advise more frequent glucose monitoring and may need temporary insulin dose adjustment.\n3. **Steroid use considerations**: Limit oral steroid use given diabetes. Intra-articular injection is preferred (less systemic glucose effect). If oral course is used, coordinate with her diabetes provider for medication adjustment.\n\n**Workplace Modifications**:\n1. **Ergonomic assessment**: Request formal evaluation of her workstation.\n2. **Specific accommodations** (under ADA as a temporary disability):\n   - Relocate files from overhead shelving to desk-height or below-shoulder-height locations\n   - Ergonomic keyboard tray and mouse positioning to avoid shoulder elevation\n   - Reduce repetitive overhead reaching tasks — reassign to colleague temporarily\n   - Allow scheduled breaks for stretching exercises (10 minutes, 2-3 times per workday)\n   - Modified keyboard/mouse position to reduce shoulder strain\n3. **Temporary work restrictions**: No overhead lifting >5 pounds with right arm. No sustained reaching above shoulder height.\n4. **Documentation**: Provide a work capacity evaluation letter specifying temporary restrictions and expected duration (3-6 months minimum).\n5. **Gradual return to full duties**: As ROM improves through the thawing phase, progressively increase work demands. Do not return to full overhead activities until at least 140 degrees of forward flexion and 40 degrees of external rotation are achieved.',
